var e=1,t=`fsa-cashflow-01`,n=`financial-statement-analysis`,r=`The Cash Flow Statement and Free Cash Flow`,i=`Three sections, one reconciliation, and the company that was profitable and cash-negative in the same year.`,a=`core`,o=[`fsa-statements-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L1`,`CAIA-L1`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`The [[cash-flow-statement|cash flow statement]] splits every cash movement into three sections, and the split is the point.

**Operating.** Cash from running the business — collections from customers less payments to suppliers, staff and tax. Sustainable cash generation lives here.

**Investing.** Buying and selling long-lived assets. [[capex|Capital expenditure]] is the big line; acquisitions and disposals sit here too.

**Financing.** Cash from and to the providers of capital — drawing or repaying debt, issuing or buying back shares, paying dividends.

**Why the split matters: cash from operations and cash from a bond issue both increase the bank balance, and they mean completely opposite things.** One is the business working; the other is a claim on the business growing. A company whose cash balance rose entirely through financing had a bad year with a good ending.

The indirect presentation, which is standard, starts from net income and adds back non-cash items — [[depreciation|depreciation]] first — then adjusts for [[working-capital|working capital]] movements. It is a reconciliation from accounting profit to cash, which is exactly what makes it informative.`},{type:`concept`,id:`free-cash-flow`,body:`**[[free-cash-flow|Free cash flow]] is the cash left after the business has paid to keep itself running and to maintain its assets. It is the money that can actually service debt or reach an owner.**

The common version: operating cash flow minus capital expenditure. Simple, and it hides one important judgement — how much of the capex was *maintenance* (replacing what wore out) and how much was *growth* (adding capacity).

That distinction changes the number materially. A business spending 35 of capex, of which 15 is maintenance and 20 is expansion, has far more discretionary cash than the headline suggests: growth capex can be stopped, maintenance capex cannot for long.

**Which is why the split is worth asking for even though it is a judgement.** Managements are usually willing to give it, and the answer tells you what the business looks like if it stops growing — which is precisely the scenario a lender cares about.

Beware the variant question of *whose* free cash flow. Cash flow available to all capital providers is before interest; cash flow to equity is after. Comparing one company's pre-interest figure with another's post-interest figure produces nonsense, and it happens constantly.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`growth-eats-cash`,body:`**Growth consumes cash, and the faster the growth the more it consumes. This is not a problem to be fixed — it is arithmetic.**

Selling more means carrying more inventory and waiting on more receivables before the cash arrives. Both are cash out now against cash in later. So a business growing 30% a year with a 70-day cash cycle is financing its own expansion out of working capital, whatever its margins look like.

The consequences are worth holding together:

**A fast-growing profitable business can be a cash consumer for years.** Perfectly healthy, and it needs funding.

**A declining business can throw off cash as working capital unwinds.** Receivables collect, inventory sells down, and the cash flow statement looks excellent in the year the business shrinks. **A one-year cash flow improvement in a shrinking company is often the [[balance-sheet|balance sheet]] liquidating, not the operations improving.**

**Which is why cash flow needs to be read next to revenue growth, always.** The same operating cash flow figure means opposite things at +30% and −10%.

The honest version for diligence: **strong cash conversion in a growing business is genuinely impressive; strong cash conversion in a shrinking one needs a second look.**`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-fcf`,body:`The same company from the previous topic. Net income 22.5, depreciation and amortisation 30, working capital increased by 20, capital expenditure 35.`,walkthrough:[`Start from net income: **22.5**.`,`Add back D&A of 30 — a real cost, but not a cash payment this period: **52.5**.`,`Subtract the 20 increase in working capital. Growth tied up cash in receivables and inventory: operating cash flow = **32.5**.`,`Subtract capital expenditure of 35: free cash flow = **−2.5**.`,`**Read that carefully. The company earned 22.5 of profit and consumed 2.5 of cash in the same year.** Nothing improper happened. Growth absorbed working capital and the business spent on assets.`,`Now split the capex: if 15 of the 35 was maintenance, free cash flow before growth spending is 32.5 − 15 = **+17.5**. The business is discretionary-cash-positive and choosing to invest.`,`**That single split turns "cash negative" into "investing", and both descriptions are accurate.** It is why the maintenance figure is worth asking for.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **compare cumulative net income with cumulative operating cash flow over three to five years.** They should be roughly comparable in a stable business. A persistent gap means revenue is being recognised well before it is collected, or costs capitalised rather than expensed — either needs an explanation.`]},{type:`table`,id:`patterns`,caption:`Reading the three sections together`,headers:[`Pattern`,`Likely story`,`What to check`],rows:[[`Operating positive, investing negative, financing negative`,`A mature business funding itself and returning capital`,`Whether maintenance capex is being deferred to flatter the figure.`],[`Operating negative, financing positive`,`Growth or distress, funded externally`,`Which one — revenue growth separates them immediately.`],[`Operating positive and rising while revenue falls`,`Working capital unwinding as the business shrinks`,`Whether receivables and inventory are simply liquidating.`],[`Operating flat, profit rising`,`Profit recognised ahead of collection`,`Receivable days, revenue recognition policy, and the notes.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Deferring payments at the period end.** Paying suppliers a week later moves cash across the reporting date and flatters operating cash flow with no change in the business. Visible in [[dpo|payable days]] jumping in the final quarter.

**Capitalising what should be expensed.** Costs moved from the [[income-statement|income statement]] to the balance sheet raise both profit and operating cash flow, because the spending is reclassified as investing. Two flattered numbers from one decision — which is why capitalisation policy sits in the notes and is worth reading.

**Selling receivables.** [[factoring|Factoring]] converts a receivable into cash now, improving operating cash flow, at a cost that sits in financing or in interest. Legitimate financing; not improved operations.

**Reading one year.** Any of the above works for a period and reverses. Multi-year cumulative comparison defeats all of them.

**Confusing [[ebitda|EBITDA]] with cash flow.** EBITDA ignores working capital, capex, interest and tax. In a working-capital-hungry, capital-intensive, levered business, that is most of the cash. The worked example above had 80 of EBITDA and −2.5 of free cash flow.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`ebitda-is-not-cash`,body:`**"EBITDA is a proxy for cash flow" is the most expensive shorthand in finance.**

Walk the gap in the worked company: EBITDA 80, less working capital 20, less capex 35, less interest 20, less tax 7.5 — and the free cash flow to equity is negative. **Every one of those deductions is a real payment that has to be made in cash.**

EBITDA is genuinely useful for what it is: a comparison of operating performance across companies with different [[capital-structure|capital structures]], tax positions and asset ages. That is a real job and it is why the measure exists.

What it is not is available money. A leverage multiple of 5.0x EBITDA sounds like five years of cash flow to repay the debt; in this company, at these numbers, it is never — because free cash flow is negative and the debt gets refinanced rather than repaid, which is the actual plan in most leveraged structures and is rarely stated as one.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask for the split of capex between maintenance and growth.** It is a judgement and management has one. It tells you what the business looks like if it stops expanding, which is the lender's scenario.

**Compare cumulative profit and cumulative operating cash flow across five years.** One number, enormous information content, and it defeats every single-period flattering technique.

**Check payable days in the final quarter against the rest of the year.** A jump means cash was moved across the reporting date rather than generated.

**Ask whether any receivables are factored or sold, and how much.** It changes what operating cash flow means and it is disclosed inconsistently.

**Read operating cash flow next to revenue growth, never alone.** Improving cash flow in a shrinking business is usually the balance sheet unwinding, and it will not repeat.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`The three sections mean different things: cash from operations is the business working, cash from financing is a claim on the business growing.`,`The worked company earned 22.5 of profit and consumed 2.5 of cash in the same year — growth in working capital and capex accounted for all of it.`,`Split capex into maintenance and growth and the same company goes from cash-negative to +17.5 of discretionary cash. Both descriptions are true; ask for the split.`,`EBITDA is not cash flow. In the worked company, working capital, capex, interest and tax turned 80 of EBITDA into negative free cash flow to equity.`]}],u=[{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q1`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:2,tags:[`cash-flow`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-fcf`,stem:`Net income 22.5, D&A 30, working capital increased by 20. What is operating cash flow?`,answer:32.5,tolerance:.3,toleranceType:`abs`,inputHint:`Add back non-cash charges, subtract the cash absorbed by working capital.`,explanation:`Note the direction of the working capital adjustment: an increase in working capital is a use of cash, so it is subtracted. Getting this sign backwards is the single most common error in building a cash flow statement, and it flatters the answer by twice the amount.`},{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:2,tags:[`cash-flow`,`free-cash-flow`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-fcf`,stem:`Operating cash flow is 32.5 and capital expenditure is 35. What is free cash flow? Give the sign.`,answer:-2.5,tolerance:.3,toleranceType:`abs`,explanation:`A profitable company with negative free cash flow is a completely ordinary situation, not a red flag on its own — but it does mean the business needs external funding to continue at this growth rate, and that dependency is the thing to understand.`},{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`cash-flow`,`capex`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-fcf`,stem:`Of the 35 of capex, 15 is maintenance. What is free cash flow before growth capital expenditure?`,answer:17.5,tolerance:.3,toleranceType:`abs`,explanation:`The same company, two very different characterisations — and this is why the split is contested in negotiations. A seller wants growth capex classified generously; a lender wants maintenance defined broadly. Both are arguing about the same number for opposite reasons.`},{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`cash-flow`,`analysis`],concept:`growth-eats-cash`,stem:`A company's revenue fell 10% and its operating cash flow improved sharply. What is the most likely explanation?`,choices:[`Management improved operational efficiency`,`Working capital is unwinding — receivables collecting and inventory selling down without being replaced`,`The company issued equity`,`Depreciation increased`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Possible, and the timing makes the simpler explanation far more likely — and efficiency gains do not usually appear in the same year revenue falls 10%.`,`Correct. Shrinking releases the cash previously tied up in the balance sheet. It is a genuine cash inflow and it is not repeatable, because a balance sheet can only be liquidated once.`,`Equity issuance is a financing flow and would not appear in operating cash flow.`,`Higher depreciation raises the add-back but does not produce a sharp improvement, and it would be visible in the income statement.`],explanation:`The general habit: read operating cash flow next to revenue growth. The identical cash flow figure means "impressive conversion" at +30% revenue growth and "the balance sheet is emptying" at −10%. Neither reading is available from the cash flow statement alone.`},{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q5`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`cash-flow`,`ebitda`,`misconception`],concept:`ebitda-is-not-cash`,stem:`"Debt of 5x EBITDA means about five years of cash flow would repay it." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — EBITDA is before working capital, capex, interest and tax, all of which must be paid in cash, so the cash actually available for repayment is far lower`,`False — because interest rates change over five years`,`True — that is what the multiple means`,`False — because EBITDA is usually adjusted upward`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. In the worked company, 80 of EBITDA became negative free cash flow after those four deductions. The multiple measures the size of the debt relative to operating earnings, not a repayment period.`,`True and secondary. Even at a fixed rate the arithmetic does not work, because the deductions come first.`,`This is the intuitive reading of the multiple and the reason it is so often misused in committee papers.`,`Adjustments make the multiple flattering, and the deeper problem is what the denominator excludes.`],explanation:`The honest description of most leveraged structures: the debt is not repaid from cash flow, it is refinanced or repaid at exit. That is a legitimate plan with a specific dependency — access to credit markets at the maturity date — and calling the multiple "five years of cash flow" hides exactly that dependency.`},{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`cash-flow`,`diligence`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`Operating cash flow was strong in Q4 and payable days jumped from 45 to 70 in the same quarter. What happened?`,choices:[`The company negotiated better supplier terms permanently`,`Payments to suppliers were delayed across the reporting date, moving cash into the period without any change in the business`,`Revenue grew strongly in Q4`,`Inventory was written down`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Possible and testable — if the terms are permanent, days stay at 70 next quarter. The one-quarter jump at the year end is the tell.`,`Correct. Stretching payables converts next period's cash outflow into this period's cash balance. It reverses immediately, and the year-end timing makes intent the natural inference.`,`Revenue growth typically consumes cash through receivables rather than improving cash flow in the same quarter.`,`A write-down is non-cash and would show as a reduced profit with an add-back, not as a payables jump.`],explanation:`The follow-up that settles it: what were payable days in the first quarter of the following year? Reversal confirms a timing exercise; persistence confirms renegotiated terms. Two data points, no argument required.`},{id:`fsa-cashflow-01-q7`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`cash-flow`,`diligence`,`on-the-job`],concept:`on-the-job`,stem:`Which single comparison is most efficient at detecting aggressive revenue recognition over time?`,choices:[`Gross margin versus peers`,`Cumulative net income versus cumulative operating cash flow over five years`,`Revenue growth versus market growth`,`Days inventory outstanding`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Margin comparison catches pricing and cost differences, and says nothing about when revenue was recognised.`,`Correct. Recognition ahead of collection shows up as profit persistently exceeding cash generation. Aggregating over five years defeats single-period timing effects, and the calculation takes a minute.`,`Outgrowing a market is interesting and can be entirely genuine.`,`Inventory days speak to stock management rather than to revenue timing.`],explanation:`This is the closest thing to a universal diagnostic in financial analysis, and it is cheap: two subtotals from statements you already have. It will not tell you what is wrong, only that something needs explaining — which is exactly what a first-pass screen should do.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
